ake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/65a899ed4375be4681c0e4598cc4769335cabb52257a886148438f4b4f16e671.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)>0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\in (-2,1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd5c48a023c450892602f94d85885db356a4d6569bc664f76e8806fb5a098947"} {"question" "q-40270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "40d53369556a3591f8a5fabb19914a539057ea8d02e671d5400829b03c8a88a4"} {"question" "q-40270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd5c48a023c450892602f94d85885db356a4d6569bc664f76e8806fb5a098947"} {"question" "q-40270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\in (-2,0)\\cup (1,\\infty )"] ["math" "x\\in (-2,1)"] ["math" "x\\in (-2,1)\\cup (2,\\infty )"] ["math" "x\\in (-\\infty ,2)"] ["math" "x\\in (1,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd5c48a023c450892602f94d85885db356a4d6569bc664f76e8806fb5a098947"} {"question" "q-40270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\in (-2,1)\\cup (2,\\infty )"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd5c48a023c450892602f94d85885db356a4d6569bc664f76e8806fb5a098947"} {"question" "q-40188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the polynomial $P(x)=x(x+3)^{4}(x-1),$ find the solution of $P(x)>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd5c48a023c450892602f94d85885db356a4d6569bc664f76e8806fb5a098947"} {"question" "q-40188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve the inequality $P(x)>0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)=x(x+3)^{4}(x-1)$ are $x=-3,x=0$ and $x=1,$ where\n\n- $x=-3$ has multiplicity $4,$ and\n\n- $x=0$ and $x=1$ are simple roots.\n\nAlso, $P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $6$ with a positive leading coefficient. So, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/6938cb283f25ccc325d7f50fcd793f1b9928025218bb6e9ca08f2d3941d16690.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)>0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-3,0)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd5c48a023c450892602f94d85885db356a4d6569bc664f76e8806fb5a098947"} {"question" "q-40188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "40d53369556a3591f8a5fabb19914a539057ea8d02e671d5400829b03c8a88a4"} {"question" "q-40188" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd5c48a023c450892602f94d85885db356a4d6569bc664f76e8806fb5a098947"} {"question" "q-40188" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\in (-3,0)"] ["math" "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)"] ["math" "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-3,0)\\cup (1,\\infty )"] ["math" "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (1,\\infty )"] ["math" "x\\in (1,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd5c48a023c450892602f94d85885db356a4d6569bc664f76e8806fb5a098947"} {"question" "q-40188" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-3,0)\\cup (1,\\infty )"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd5c48a023c450892602f94d85885db356a4d6569bc664f76e8806fb5a098947"} {"question" "q-279291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $f(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2},$ solve $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\n$x\\in {$\n{{field-1}}$}$\n$⋃[$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd5c48a023c450892602f94d85885db356a4d6569bc664f76e8806fb5a098947"} {"question" "q-279291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=f(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}-3x+2) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}-x-2x+2) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}[(x(x-1)-2(x-1)] \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x-1)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial are $x=0$, $x=1$ and $x=2,$ where\n\n- $x=0$ has multiplicity $2,$ and\n\n- $x=1$ and $x=2$ are simple roots.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/baf599e732aa524409881d4bc7598c3f998cffe116ca1cdcd76fb6e6bd49a078.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis or touches this axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is\n\n$$\nx\\in {0}⋃[1,2].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd5c48a023c450892602f94d85885db356a4d6569bc664f76e8806fb5a098947"} {"question" "q-279291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "40d53369556a3591f8a5fabb19914a539057ea8d02e671d5400829b03c8a88a4"} {"question" "q-279291" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd5c48a023c450892602f94d85885db356a4d6569bc664f76e8806fb5a098947"} {"question" "q-279291" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd5c48a023c450892602f94d85885db356a4d6569bc664f76e8806fb5a098947"} {"question" "q-279291" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/state.json" "file_sha256" "fd5c48a023c450892602f94d85885db356a4d6569bc664f76e8806fb5a098947"} {"question" "q-279291" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" 