alues of $x$ for which the curve $y=p(x)$ lies below the $x$-axis. These intervals are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/141519a0d0abc0d1545d940aba1fd52b15e96d81b0c10560960df2710da4937f.png)\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is\n\n$$\nx \\in (-\\infty, -3) \\cup (-3, -1) \\cup (0,2).\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** Notice that this is a strict inequality, so we do not include the roots in our solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/example-5819.json" "file_sha256" "669c2972a8a396a34d07bce530d2a57c11c7a36bf96a6b14e0781ebce6b2d700"} {"question" "e-5821" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the polynomial $P(x) = x^3(x+2)^2(x-2),$ find the solution of $P(x) \\geq 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/example-5821.json" "file_sha256" "ffdc6c088590520c0138eb96df52908d2314da0334470193f7213701d1dcdf3e"} {"question" "e-5821" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve the inequality $P(x) \\geq 0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x) = x^3(x+2)^2(x-2)$ are $x=0$ and $x= \\pm{2},$ where\n\n- $x=-2$ has multiplicity $2,$\n\n- $x=0$ has multiplicity $3,$ and\n\n- $x=2$ is a simple root.\n\nAlso, $P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $6$ with a positive leading coefficient. So, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/435846c9b0efa313b76b0e13689fb2225209d7ee1ea52e332a65bd676d97cc8f.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x) \\geq 0,$ we look for the parts of the curve that are above the $x$-axis or touching the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x \\in (-\\infty,0] \\cup [2, \\infty).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/example-5821.json" "file_sha256" "ffdc6c088590520c0138eb96df52908d2314da0334470193f7213701d1dcdf3e"} {"question" "e-5829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given $f(x) = x^3 - 9x,$ solve $f(x) \\gt 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/example-5829.json" "file_sha256" "462eca5c44b30b0e9f4b47b571010ed9ce03a65940fe39927df0a03e249e1901"} {"question" "e-5829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve the inequality $f(x) \\gt 0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y = f(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}-9x \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =x(x+3)(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $f(x) = x^3 - 9x$ are $x= - 3,$ $x=0,$ and $x = 3,$ which are all simple roots.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/91e0e81b98e7f89796c277854bec6ce0240c797fc0c3d1eda3f33ce4bc9c85a6.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $f(x) \\gt 0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is \n\n$$\nx \\in (-3,0) \\cup (3,\\infty).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/example-5829.json" "file_sha256" "462eca5c44b30b0e9f4b47b571010ed9ce03a65940fe39927df0a03e249e1901"}] "usage" {"e-5819" [] "q-40185" [] "q-40270" [] "e-5821" [] "e-5829" []} "basis" 1129 "retractions" [[17592186134184 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2147/Source/Images/q-40185.png)\n\nGiven the sketch of a polynomial $p(x)$ above, find the solution to $p(x) < 0$."] [17592186134203 :question/problem "Given the polynomial $P(x) = (x + 2)(x - 1)(x - 2)$, find the solution of $P(x) > 0$."] [17592186133544 :question/problem "Given the sketch of the polynomial $y = p(x)$ shown above, find the solution to $p(x) < 0$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2147/Source/Images/d83a13bf5ff8ffe872e5b3c6e4a0f77b.png)"] [17592186133544 :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality $p(x) < 0$, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=p(x)$ lies below the $x$-axis. These intervals are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2147/Source/Images/a34e9e5ebc457ab4ff0ab4ce9004f44f.png)\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is\n$x \\in (-\\infty, -3) \\cup (-3, -1) \\cup (0,2)$.\n\n**Watch out!** Notice that this is a strict inequality, so we do not include the roots in our solution."] [17592186133545 :question/problem "Given the polynomial $P(x) = x^3(x+2)^2(x-2)$, find the solution of $P(x) \\geq 0$."] [17592186133545 :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality\n\n$$\nP(x) \\geq 0\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nThe roots of the polynomial\n\n$$\nP(x) = x^3(x+2)^2(x-2)\n$$\n\nare $x=0$ and $x= \\pm{2}$, where\n\n- $x=-2$ has multiplicity $2$,\n- $x=0$ has multiplicity $3$, and\n- $x=2$ is a simple root.\n\nAlso, $P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $6$ with a positive leading coefficient. So, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2147/Source/Images/6b432fef92363fb20bc112fcae6d7bc7.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving\n\n$$\nP(x) \\geq 0\n$$\n\nwe look for the parts of the curve that are above the $x$-axis or touching the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $[MATH: x \\in (-\\infty,0] \\cup [2, \\infty).]$"] [17592186133548 :question/problem "Given $f(x) = x^3 - 9x$, solve $f(x) \\gt 0$."] [17592186133548 :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality $f(x) \\gt 0$, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y = f(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= x^{3} - 9x \\\\\n&= x(x^{2} - 9) \\\\\n&= x(x + 3)(x - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^3 - 9x\n$$\n\nare $x=-3$, $x=0$, and $x = 3$, which are all simple roots.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2147/Source/Images/539d1de5f6c1505dc8a8fbe9db23f6b8.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $f(x) \\gt 0$, we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is\n$x \\in (-3,0) \\cup (3,\\infty)$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186131547 17592186131548 17592186131549 17592186131550 17592186131551 17592186131661 17592186131662 17592186131663 17592186131664 17592186131665] "immutable_fields" [[17592186131546 :answer-field/correct] [17592186131546 :answer-field/id] [17592186131546 :answer-field/key] [17592186131546 :answer-field/type] [17592186131660 :answer-field/correct] [17592186131660 :answer-field/id] [17592186131660 :answer-field/key] [17592186131660 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
